ONTAP S3 reverse proxy on the core Envoy Gateway.

Browsers PUT documents straight to the URL EDP returns, and the ONTAP data LIF
lives on the storage network, so the presigned endpoint has to be published
through the gateway.

Choices below that are deliberate:
  - discovery.k8s.io/v1 EndpointSlice rather than the deprecated core/v1 Endpoints.
  - everything in the edp namespace, so no ReferenceGrant is needed.
  - upstream TLS through an envoy-gateway Backend, or a BackendTLSPolicy when a CA
    is supplied; there are no sidecars on the gateway path here.
  - tls.alpnProtocols: [http/1.1] on the Backend. Envoy Gateway builds the upstream
    cluster with auto_config, so a TLS backend that advertises h2 in ALPN gets
    HTTP/2, and ONTAP S3 breaks under that upgrade. Plaintext upstreams stay
    HTTP/1.1 by default, and neither a Backend appProtocols entry nor a Service
    appProtocol of h2c is set below.

Two rules below are load-bearing for SigV4 and must not be "improved":
  - no URLRewrite hostname filter: the Host header is part of the signature.
  - in path mode the PathPrefix must equal edp_s3_path_prefix, because
    src/edp/app/utils.py re-injects that prefix after signing and the gateway
    has to strip it again.

They are necessary but not sufficient: the listener also runs with normalize_path,
merge_slashes and path_with_escaped_slashes_action, which rewrite a signed path before
it reaches ONTAP and produce SignatureDoesNotMatch. This template cannot fix that -
the first two are per-listener (ClientTrafficPolicy on the core installer's Gateway)
and normalize_path has no Gateway API knob at all. /api/presignedUrl rejects object
names that would be rewritten (src/edp/app/utils.py::gateway_unsafe_object_name_segments),
and tasks/ontap_s3_validate.yaml reports whether the listener carries the policy.
#}
{% set _svc = edp_ontap_s3_backend_name %}
{% set _port = _edp_ontap_s3_port | int %}
{% set _tls_mode = _edp_ontap_s3_tls_mode %}
{% set _lif = _edp_ontap_s3_lif | string | trim %}
{% set _port_name = 'http' if _tls_mode == 'plaintext' else 'https' %}
{% set _path_mode = _edp_s3_path_mode | bool %}
{% set _path_prefix = edp_s3_path_prefix | default(edp_default_s3_path_prefix, true) %}
{# Compared as text so the expression holds for a real boolean and for the string an
   Ansible fact may carry. #}
{% set _lif_is_ipv4 = (_edp_ontap_s3_lif_is_ipv4 | string | lower) == 'true' %}
{% if _tls_mode != 'insecure' %}
{# Only the ca and plaintext modes route through the Service; the insecure mode
   points backendRefs at the envoy-gateway Backend, which carries its own
   endpoint, so emitting these two objects there would leave dead ones behind. #}
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: "{{ _svc }}"
  namespace: "{{ edp_namespace }}"
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: "{{ _svc }}"
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: ansible
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  # Selectorless: the endpoints are maintained by the EndpointSlice below.
  ports:
    - name: "{{ _port_name }}"
      protocol: TCP
      port: {{ _port }}
      targetPort: {{ _port }}
---
apiVersion: discovery.k8s.io/v1
kind: EndpointSlice
metadata:
  name: "{{ _svc }}"
  namespace: "{{ edp_namespace }}"
  labels:
    kubernetes.io/service-name: "{{ _svc }}"
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: ansible
addressType: IPv4
endpoints:
  - addresses:
      - "{{ _lif }}"
    conditions:
      ready: true
ports:
  - name: "{{ _port_name }}"
    protocol: TCP
    port: {{ _port }}
{% endif %}
{% if _tls_mode == 'insecure' %}
---
# ONTAP serves the S3 endpoint with its own certificate, which is normally
# self-signed and issued to the SVM rather than to the data LIF address.
apiVersion: gateway.envoyproxy.io/v1alpha1
kind: Backend
metadata:
  name: "{{ _svc }}"
  namespace: "{{ edp_namespace }}"
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: ansible
spec:
  endpoints:
{% if _lif_is_ipv4 %}
    - ip:
        address: "{{ _lif }}"
        port: {{ _port }}
{% else %}
    # The Backend API resolves a name itself, so this mode also accepts the
    # hostname form of a data LIF that the core installer passes through verbatim.
    - fqdn:
        hostname: "{{ _lif }}"
        port: {{ _port }}
{% endif %}
  tls:
    insecureSkipVerify: true
    # Envoy Gateway defaults a Backend to ALPN [h2, http/1.1] and the cluster is
    # built with auto_config, so an upstream advertising h2 gets HTTP/2. ONTAP S3
    # breaks under that upgrade; offering only http/1.1 is the Gateway API
    # equivalent of Istio's h2UpgradePolicy: DO_NOT_UPGRADE.
    alpnProtocols:
      - "http/1.1"
{% elif _tls_mode == 'ca' %}
---
# v1 is served by every Gateway API bundle from v1.4 on, and it is the only
# version bundles from v1.5 serve: v1alpha3 stays listed in the CRD but with
# served: false, so applying it fails with "no matches for kind". The same
# spec shape applies to both. tasks/ontap_s3_proxy.yaml checks the CRD serves v1
# before applying.
#
# Gateway API has no ALPN control, so this mode cannot pin the upstream to
# HTTP/1.1 the way the Backend above does. Use edp_ontap_s3_tls_mode: insecure or
# plaintext if the ONTAP S3 server negotiates h2 and then misbehaves.
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: BackendTLSPolicy
metadata:
  name: "{{ _svc }}-tls"
  namespace: "{{ edp_namespace }}"
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: ansible
spec:
  targetRefs:
    - group: ""
      kind: Service
      name: "{{ _svc }}"
  validation:
    hostname: "{{ edp_ontap_s3_sni_hostname | default('', true) or _lif }}"
    caCertificateRefs:
      - group: ""
        kind: ConfigMap
        name: "{{ edp_ontap_s3_ca_configmap_name }}"
{% endif %}
---
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata:
  name: "{{ edp_ontap_s3_route_name }}"
  namespace: "{{ edp_namespace }}"
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: "{{ _svc }}"
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: ansible
spec:
  parentRefs:
    # tasks/s3_listener_section.yaml picks the listener whose hostname is exactly
    # this route's hostname: the core installer's dedicated S3 listener in
    # subdomain mode, else the 'ontap-s3' ListenerSet on a gateway that accepts
    # them, else the wildcard listener; the apex listener in path mode. Getting
    # this wrong leaves the route Accepted=False with reason
    # NoMatchingListenerHostname. The default below is the same decision without
    # a cluster to ask, for a gateway that predates ListenerSets.
    - {{ _edp_s3_parent | default({'name': gateway_name, 'namespace': gateway_namespace,
                                    'sectionName': 'https-apex' if _path_mode else gateway_s3_listener_name}, true) | to_json }}
  hostnames:
    - "{{ _edp_s3_host }}"
  rules:
    - matches:
        - path:
            type: PathPrefix
{% if _path_mode %}
            value: "{{ _path_prefix }}"
      filters:
        - type: URLRewrite
          urlRewrite:
            path:
              type: ReplacePrefixMatch
              replacePrefixMatch: "/"
{% else %}
            value: "/"
{% endif %}
      timeouts:
        request: "{{ edp_ontap_s3_request_timeout }}"
        backendRequest: "{{ edp_ontap_s3_request_timeout }}"
      backendRefs:
{% if _tls_mode == 'insecure' %}
        - group: gateway.envoyproxy.io
          kind: Backend
          name: "{{ _svc }}"
{% else %}
        - name: "{{ _svc }}"
          port: {{ _port }}
{% endif %}
---
# Bypass the gateway-level JWT policy for the S3 API route. The core installer
# attaches SecurityPolicy/gateway-auth to the whole Gateway
# (roles/envoy_gateway/templates/security-policy.yaml.j2 targets kind: Gateway),
# and S3 clients present SigV4 signatures, not OIDC tokens. Without this every
# upload returns 401. Empty spec = no authentication for this route.
apiVersion: gateway.envoyproxy.io/v1alpha1
kind: SecurityPolicy
metadata:
  name: "{{ _svc }}-no-auth"
  namespace: "{{ edp_namespace }}"
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: ansible
spec:
  targetRefs:
    - group: gateway.networking.k8s.io
      kind: HTTPRoute
      name: "{{ edp_ontap_s3_route_name }}"
{% if not _path_mode and edp_ontap_s3_cors_allow_origins %}
  # ONTAP S3 has no CORS configuration and answers the browser's preflight without
  # Access-Control-* headers, so the gateway supplies them and terminates the OPTIONS
  # here. Not needed in routing_mode: path, where uploads are same-origin.
  # Presigned URLs carry the signature in the query string, so the browser only sends
  # the content headers it computes itself. ETag has to be exposed for the browser to
  # confirm each part of a multipart upload.
  cors:
    allowOrigins:
{% for _origin in edp_ontap_s3_cors_allow_origins %}
      - "{{ _origin }}"
{% endfor %}
    allowMethods: [GET, PUT, POST, DELETE, HEAD, OPTIONS]
    allowHeaders: [content-type, content-md5, x-amz-content-sha256, x-amz-date, x-amz-user-agent]
    exposeHeaders: [ETag, x-amz-request-id, x-amz-version-id]
    maxAge: "1h"
{% endif %}
